A||||8||Matt88||2.8 and 3.1 engines||||1||
Z||000000||Matt88||01-01-2001||09:42 PM||||I have an '88 GT with 70k miles.  It runs great, but I'd like a few extra ponies.  I've built several Chev. V8's and I'd like to try the V6; the problem is, where I live we have to pass emissions tests.  Engine swaps are generally a no-go situation if I want to register my car again.<P>Question is, are the 2.8 and 3.1 blocks basically the same?  I understand the crank stroke is different, but what about using the 2.8 distributor?  Computer problems?  Has anyone used any of Crane's computer compatible cams?  Other problems?||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||01-01-2001||10:00 PM||||Blows my hopes about Oregon. I might be moving to Roseburg this summer, and was hoping/planning my very soon engine swap, exhaust change on a no inspections, emmisions thing. Plus, you can't even pump your own gas in that state! Guess its another reason to stay here in Ohio. Ever think about the 3.4. It can be installed and looks no different from the stock 2.8.<P>Figure I'd answer your question. 2.8 and 3.1 blocks are the same. The only difference is the 3.1 crank has a longer throw, and the 3.1 wrist pins attach higher on the piston.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||01-01-2001||10:43 PM||||The Crane camshafts will pass emissions along with the GM 12363220 camshaft, which is a great camshaft for the stroker motors.<P>Get the 3.4-liter motor. It's a better engine.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||01-01-2001||10:48 PM||||I see you are a new member so if you don't know already the 2.8, 3.1, and 3.4-liter engines respond very well to traditional modifications like the ones done with small block Chevy's.<P>You can do a search in the archives here on the forum and find a wealth of information about these engines.<P>I have Dyno2000 and Dragstrip2000 so if you would like me to test an engine combination and then tell you how fast it would be in your car I can do that for you.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||David Gamache||01-03-2001||02:38 PM||||This weekend I finally picked up a 3.1L block from a 92 pontiac. The block, so far, appears to be the same. The timing cover from the 2.8 is compatable to the 3.1 and the trans bolts on in the same pattern. The flexplate/flywheel for the 3.1 can be used from a 88 model year fiero (due to balance as mentioned earlier by another member). The distributor from the 2.8 slides right into the 3.1 even the hold down bolt and bracket is the same. The cam part # for the 2.8 and the 3.1 show to be the same. The oil pan lip on the 3.1 is different, so the oil pan has to be used from a 2.8 but the 3.1 does use the same oil pan gasket # as the 2.8.  The oil dip stick is in the same place but mounts a little different into the block of a 3.1. The pistons on the 3.1 are dished as this motor had aluminum heads but so did an 89 model year I looked at. I have not tried to put the fiero heads from a 2.8 on the 3.1 yet because I do not have an extra set of heads to test the fit at this time. The heads should fit, although the head gasket part # is different because of the aluminum heads in stead of the cast iron on the 2.8. Maybe someone can confirm the heads and intake bolt on the same? I do believe they will fit fine though. Hope that helps.     ||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||01-03-2001||02:51 PM||||Hi Matt,<P>I live in California and I can't believe Oregon is tighter than us. We can swap the same year or newer engine in and get it blessed (for free) provided we:<P>1. Install a same year or new engine. Doesn't have to be the same engine. We can put a 3800 Buick or TPI V8 in a Fiero.<BR>2. All emissions equipment.<BR>3. Use only engines from cars, not trucks. Strange, but you can't put a car engine in a truck either.<BR>4. The engine and trans combination must have been certified in California (i.e. no 3800's on Getrags). It doesn't have to be the matching automatic or manual but it's a good idea if it uses an E trans.<P>You should check with your test stations and see if they have a swap program. We call it an "Engine Conversion".<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-03-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000006||mshill||01-03-2001||03:15 PM||||terry should know, saw/heard his 3.8s/c run on New Years day.  It is sweet.  <BR>Terry, thanks for the engine, and your gloves found their way into my truck (imagine that).<BR>I'll find a way to get them back to you, I just may have to drive my 86SE up there after I get the rear swaybar put on it.  Niiiiice road to drive good handling cars on.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Matt88||01-03-2001||03:46 PM||||Thanks for all the replies.  If the alum. heads for 3.1 will bolt up to the 2.8 intake and exhaust, it would seem using the alum. heads would be better from a performance standpoint.  Although the 3.1 has dished pistons, the smaller chamber heads in addition to the chamber design would actually increase power. In addition, alum. heads are lighter plus you should be able to run a higher compression ratio without problems with detonation - they run cooler.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Matt88||01-03-2001||03:52 PM||||Also, regarding the emissions requirements.  Yes you can do engine swaps in Oregon, but generally the requirements follow the model year of the body or the engine.  If it is pretty much a straight swap - like I imagine a 3.1 for a 2.8, nobody really cares.  But if I were to install a Northstar V8 - I'd spend more time and money trying to pass the emissions requirements than is reasonable.  Anyway, I don't need that much power - 15 to 20 additional ponies in the midrange would be great!||||reg||7
